<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Via Augusta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Via Augusta was the longest Roman road in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>, running about 1,500 kilometres from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarraco">Tarraco</a> and Valentia and then inland to Corduba and Gades. Rebuilt under Augustus, after whom it was named, on the line of older routes, it shaped the main travel routes of eastern and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> for centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/via-augusta.jpg" alt="Via Augusta" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Year of the dragon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spain.Catalonia.Roda.de.Bara.Arc.Bera.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
